.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt PROTECT 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm protect
.Nd protect processes from being killed when memory runs out
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl i
.Op Fl a Ar adj
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Op Fl cdi
.Op Fl a Ar adj
.Fl g Ar pgrp | Fl p Ar pid
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sets the OOM adjustment of processes
.Pq Xr procctl 2 ,
which decides which process the system kills when it runs out of memory.
By default it sets \-1000: the processes are never chosen.
In the first form
.Nm
sets it for itself and executes
.Ar command ,
which keeps it.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-a adj"
.It Fl a Ar adj
Set
.Ar adj
instead, from \-1000 to 1000: a process's pages count
(1000 +
.Ar adj )
/ 1000 times when the victim is chosen, so 1000 makes a process the likeliest
to be killed.
.It Fl c
Clear the protection: set 0.
.It Fl d
Also set it for every descendant of the processes.
.It Fl g Ar pgrp
Set it for the processes in process group
.Ar pgrp .
.It Fl i
Accepted for compatibility: a new process always takes its parent's
adjustment.
.It Fl p Ar pid
Set it for process
.Ar pid .
.El
.Pp
Only the superuser may lower an adjustment.
The current value is in
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /oom_adj .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
When
.Ar command
is run, the exit status is its; 127 if it can't be found, and 126 if it can't
be executed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Never kill the shell this command starts:
.Dl # protect sh
.Pp
Make a large build the first to go:
.Dl $ protect -a 1000 make -j8
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr procctl 2
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command first appeared in FreeBSD 10.1.
OxideBSD's takes FreeBSD's options and adds
.Fl a ;
it first appeared in OxideBSD 0.3.0.
